# AIUI Animation and Transform Reference

Use this reference with [WXSS](./wxss.md) for keyframes, event-driven replay,
and 2D motion. Prefer transform and opacity for frequent animation; size and
typography changes require layout.

## Concurrent Animations

```css
.status {
  animation: fade-in 300ms ease-out, pulse 900ms ease-in-out infinite;
  transition: background-color 200ms ease;
}
@keyframes fade-in {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to { opacity: 1; }
}
@keyframes pulse {
  from { transform: scale(1); }
  50% { transform: scale(1.05); }
  to { transform: scale(1); }
}
```

Comma-separated animations have independent timing. Shorter duration, delay,
and related lists repeat; entries beyond the animation-name count are ignored.
Later contributing animations win on a shared property. Active transitions
override keyframes; ordinary `!important` declarations can override keyframes.

Unrelated `setData()` or style changes preserve playback progress. Removing
and re-adding an animation starts new playback. Completed `forwards` / `both`
effects retain their final values without continuously requesting frames.

## Timing and Events

- Duration / delay use `ms` or `s`; delay may be negative, duration may not.
- Iteration count accepts numbers or `infinite`; direction is `normal`, `reverse`, `alternate`, or `alternate-reverse`.
- Fill mode is `none`, `forwards`, `backwards`, or `both`.
- Timing supports `linear`, the `ease` family, `cubic-bezier()`, `step-start`, `step-end`, and `steps()`. `steps(n, jump-none)` requires `n >= 2`; other steps require positive integers.
- Zero duration or iteration count completes after delay without continuous playback.

```xml
<view class="status" bindanimationstart="onAnimation"
  bindanimationiteration="onAnimation" bindanimationend="onAnimation"
  bindanimationcancel="onAnimation" />
```

```javascript
export default {
  data: { completed: false },
  onAnimation(event) {
    console.log(event.animationName, event.elapsedTime, event.pseudoElement);
    if (event.type === 'animationend') this.setData({ completed: true });
  },
};
```

Events bubble to the Page / Widget and expose `animationName`, `elapsedTime`
in seconds excluding delay, and `pseudoElement` (`''`, `'::before'`, or
`'::after'`), also mirrored in `detail`. Use template `bind` / `catch`,
`addEventListener()`, or `onanimation...`; `stopPropagation()` stops bubbling.
Events are not cancelable. Callbacks may call `setData()` synchronously.

Removing an active animation/node or applying `display: none` fires one
`animationcancel`. Closing a Page / Widget cancels animations before
`onUnload` / `onDestroy`. Offscreen pauses and temporarily hidden Pages preserve
progress rather than restarting playback.

## Generated Text Decorations

Only single-line `<text>` supports generated `::before` / `::after` boxes.
`content` accepts quoted strings or `attr(name)`; missing attributes produce
empty strings, and `normal` / `none` generate no content. Typed `attr()` and
fallback arguments are unsupported. Use `content: ''` for an empty decoration.

`::before` anchors at the text start, `::after` at its end, both on the text
midline. These are generated boxes, not Entity/layout nodes; selectors cannot
return them as Entities.

```xml
<text class="streaming-text">{{answer}}</text>
```

```css
.streaming-text {
  position: relative;
  event-trigger: --text-update textchange;
}
.streaming-text::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  left: 100%;
  top: 50%;
  width: 6px;
  height: 6px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background-color: #74f7c5;
  animation: spark 900ms ease-out;
  animation-trigger: --text-update replay;
  -ink-animation-trigger-instance-limit: 5;
}
@keyframes spark {
  from { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
  to { opacity: 0; transform: translate(-50%, -28px) scale(0.35); }
}
```

`textchange` observes final bound text changes; `attributechange` observes
attribute-generated content changes. Initial mounting does not trigger replay;
multiple updates before one layout coalesce. `animation-trigger: --name replay`
restarts generated-box playback on the owning text. One instance is retained by
default; `-ink-animation-trigger-instance-limit` accepts integers `1`–`32` and
discards the oldest at the limit. Each instance captures its post-layout anchor
and animates only transform/opacity, so later text updates do not move older
effects. Use the bounded [random values](./wxss.md#calculated-and-random-values)
subset to vary replay endpoints, with literal fallbacks.

## 2D Transforms and Interpolation

`transform` supports `matrix()`, `translate()` / X / Y, `rotate()`, `scale()` /
X / Y, and `skew()` / X / Y. Independent `translate`, `rotate`, and `scale`
compose in that order before `transform`. For example:

```css
.badge {
  translate: 8px 25%;
  rotate: 0.125turn;
  scale: 120% 0.9;
  transform-origin: left center;
}
```

Sizes/insets, margin/padding, border widths, font-size/line-height, colors,
radii, background-position, visibility, opacity, and 2D transform/origin can
animate. Keep endpoint transform types and order compatible for continuous
interpolation; incompatible values switch at the interval midpoint.

3D transforms, perspective, a third transform-origin component, and
`animation-play-state` are unsupported. Min/max sizes, flex/grid controls, gap,
shadow, filter, clip, and outline do not interpolate.

# A2UI

Use `a2ui` to turn structured instructions generated by an AI agent into a visible interface. It is useful when the agent decides the UI at runtime, such as generating a result card, list, or chart for a user's request.

If the interface structure is fixed, regular components such as `view` and `text` are simpler.

## Display an Initial Interface

Give the component a unique `id`, then pass A2UI instructions through `commands`. The component processes `commands` only once, during its initial render.

```xml
<a2ui id="answer" commands="{{initialCommands}}"></a2ui>
```

```javascript
export default {
  data: {
    initialCommands: JSON.stringify([
      {
        version: 'v0.9',
        createSurface: { surfaceId: 'main' }
      },
      {
        version: 'v0.9',
        updateComponents: {
          surfaceId: 'main',
          components: [
            { id: 'title', component: 'Text', text: 'Today: sunny' }
          ]
        }
      }
    ])
  }
};
```

`commands` accepts a string, so use `JSON.stringify()` to convert the command array to JSON text.

## Update the Displayed Interface

When a new AI result arrives, create a context using the component's `id`, then call `write()`:

```javascript
const context = a2ui.createA2UIContext('answer');

if (context) {
  context.write(JSON.stringify([
    {
      version: 'v0.9',
      updateComponents: {
        surfaceId: 'main',
        components: [
          { id: 'title', component: 'Text', text: 'Today: cloudy' }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]));
}
```

`createA2UIContext()` returns `null` when the `id` cannot be found, so check the result before using it.

## Receive AI Output in Chunks

If the AI response arrives progressively, create a writer and pass each string chunk to it. Always call `close()` at the end so the component can finish processing the remaining content.

```javascript
const context = a2ui.createA2UIContext('answer');

if (context) {
  const writer = context.startStream();
  writer.writeChunk('{"version":"v0.9","createSurface":');
  writer.writeChunk('{"surfaceId":"stream-result"}}');
  writer.close();
}
```

Call `context.clear()` when the generated interface should be removed.

## Properties

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | String | - | Component identifier used by `a2ui.createA2UIContext()`. Required for dynamic updates. |
| `commands` | String | - | A2UI JSON instructions processed once during the initial render. |
| `default-chart-animate` | Boolean | `true` | Whether charts created by A2UI animate by default when the instruction does not specify animation. `defaultChartAnimate` is also accepted. |

## JavaScript Methods

| Method | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `a2ui.createA2UIContext(id)` | Returns the context for a component `id`, or `null` when it cannot be found. |
| `context.write(data)` | Updates the interface using a complete JSON string. |
| `context.startStream()` | Starts a chunked update and returns a writer. |
| `writer.writeChunk(chunk)` | Writes one string chunk. |
| `writer.close()` | Finishes the current chunked update. |
| `context.clear()` | Clears the generated interface. |

`a2ui` does not provide `agent-id`, `session-id`, or `bindmessage`. Manage the agent session and incoming messages in the page or an Agent Worker, then pass the resulting A2UI instructions to this component.

# Button

Use `button` to create a clickable action, such as submitting a form, confirming a selection, or reloading content.

## Respond to a Tap

Use `bindtap` to bind a handler from the page:

```xml
<button bindtap="handleSubmit">Submit</button>
```

```javascript
export default {
  handleSubmit() {
    console.log('The user tapped Submit');
  }
};
```

The button centers its child content horizontally and vertically by default. Use CSS to define its background, border, and text color:

```css
button {
  padding: 12px 20px;
  color: #ffffff;
  background-color: #07c160;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
```

`button` does not add a component-specific touch animation. Add pressed feedback with styles that match your application.

# Swiper

`swiper` and `swiper-item` are view-container tags for organizing paged content. They currently provide content grouping and CSS layout, but they do not create a carousel automatically.

## Organize Paged Content

```xml
<swiper class="swiper">
  <swiper-item class="page"><text>Page one</text></swiper-item>
  <swiper-item class="page"><text>Page two</text></swiper-item>
</swiper>
```

```css
.swiper {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
}

.page {
  width: 100%;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
```

## Current Behavior

`swiper` and `swiper-item` currently use the same basic container implementation as `view`. They support child nodes and CSS layout, but do not provide built-in pagination state, autoplay, indicators, or change events. Implement those behaviors in page logic and styles.

If you only need a regular horizontal layout, `view` is usually easier to understand. Choose `swiper` when its name helps communicate that the children represent separate pages.

# AIUI Framework Reference

Use this reference for project structure, registration, Pages, Widgets, Agent Workers, SFC files, and agent configuration.

## Project Structure

```text
agent/
├── AGENTS.md
├── app.json
├── app.js
├── pages/
│   └── index/index.ink
├── widgets/
│   └── weather/index.ink
├── workers/
│   └── sync.js
├── components/
└── assets/
```

- `AGENTS.md` describes identity and requested capabilities.
- `app.json` declares Pages, Widgets, Agent Workers, and window configuration.
- `app.js` default-exports agent lifecycle and global data.
- Paths in configuration omit the `.ink` extension where shown.

## Registration

All logic entry files use an ES module default export:

```javascript
export default {
  data: { ready: false },
  onLoad() {
    this.setData({ ready: true });
  },
};
```

Never generate `App({...})`, `Page({...})`, or `Widget({...})`.

## Page Declaration

Declare Page routes in order; the first is the default Page:

```json
{
  "pages": ["pages/index/index", "pages/detail/index"]
}
```

A Page supports either of these equivalent source structures:

- **Multi-file Page**: four files with the same base path and the extensions `.json`, `.wxml`, `.wxss`, and `.js`.
- **Single-file Page**: one `.ink` file containing the same configuration, template, style, and logic concerns.

Do not mix the two forms for the same route.

The top-level Page schema describes render-time input:

```json
{
  "description": "Displays weather for a city.",
  "schema": {
    "data": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "city": { "type": "string" },
        "temperature": { "type": "number" }
      },
      "required": ["city", "temperature"]
    }
  }
}
```

Keep `description` observable and define every required input in `schema.data`.

## Multi-file Page

For the route `pages/weather/index`, create all four files together:

```text
pages/weather/
├── index.json
├── index.wxml
├── index.wxss
└── index.js
```

`index.json` contains Page configuration and render-input metadata:

```json
{
  "navigationBarTitleText": "Weather",
  "description": "Displays weather for a city.",
  "schema": {
    "data": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "city": { "type": "string" },
        "temperature": { "type": "number" }
      },
      "required": ["city", "temperature"]
    }
  }
}
```

`index.wxml` contains the Page template. It does not need an outer `<page>` block:

```xml
<view class="weather">
  <text>{{city}}</text>
  <text>{{temperature}}°C</text>
</view>
```

`index.wxss` contains Page-local styles:

```css
.weather {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding: var(--spacing-md);
}
```

`index.js` default-exports Page state, lifecycle, and handlers:

```javascript
export default {
  data: {
    city: '',
    temperature: 0,
  },
  onLoad(query) {
    this.setData({ city: query.city || this.data.city });
  },
};
```

The four filenames must share the same basename. The corresponding `app.json` route remains extensionless: `"pages/weather/index"`.

## Single-file `.ink`

```html
<script def>
{
  "navigationBarTitleText": "Weather"
}
</script>

<script setup>
export default {
  data: { city: 'Hangzhou' },
};
</script>

<page>
  <view class="page"><text>{{city}}</text></view>
</page>

<style>
.page { display: flex; }
</style>
```

- `<script def>` contains JSON configuration, not executable JavaScript.
- `<script setup>` contains one default-exported logic object.
- `<page>` or `<widget>` is the sole interface root.
- `<style>` contains page-local WXSS/CSS.

## Data Binding and the Current Tree

Patch only changed fields with `setData(patch, callback?)`. Its callback may call
`setData()` again; query current nodes there when work depends on the update.
Array `.length` bindings are supported.

Each `ink:for` iteration has its own item/index scope. Name nested-loop variables
explicitly when outer values are needed. On a node with both `ink:for` and
`ink:if`, the condition evaluates in the current item's scope. Keep
`ink:if` / `ink:elif` / `ink:else` branches adjacent. Inactive branches are absent
from the rendered tree; `hidden="{{isHidden}}"` retains the node and binds a
boolean visibility state. After list, condition, or attribute updates, query the
current tree rather than retaining an Entity removed by a condition.

## Widget Development

AIUI supports two Widget families on Rokid Glasses:

| Family | Occupied grid cells | Current Glasses size |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| `1x1` | 1 | `239 × 140` |
| `1x2` | 2 adjacent cells | `480 × 140` |

Rokid Glasses provide four Widget grid cells. `family` is a size category, not a fixed canvas contract. Use Flexbox, percentages, available space, padding, and content-driven sizing. Never hardcode a Widget root to `239px × 140px`.

Declare the Widget in `app.json`. When using `assets/minimal-widget.ink`, add
its matching `app.json.widgets` entry before packaging:

```json
{
  "widgets": [
    {
      "path": "widgets/weather/index",
      "family": "1x2",
      "placement": "overlay",
      "displayName": "Weather",
      "description": "Shows current weather."
    }
  ]
}
```

The same family must appear in the Widget file:

```html
<script def>
{ "widget": { "family": "1x2" } }
</script>

<script setup>
export default {
  data: { temperature: 24 },
  refresh() {
    this.setData({ temperature: this.data.temperature + 1 });
  },
};
</script>

<widget>
  <view class="widget" bindtap="refresh">
    <text>{{temperature}}°C</text>
  </view>
</widget>

<style>
.widget {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
</style>
```

Widget callbacks are `onCreate(options)`, `onAttach()`, `onDetach()`, and `onDestroy()`. Attach and detach may repeat. Widgets do not use Page lifecycle callbacks or Page-only `enableWorldAwareness()` and `finish()`.

`displayName` and `description` are required default metadata. `placement` is
`persistent` by default; use `overlay` for a temporary Widget opened through
`window.open()`. Keep `placement` in `app.json`, not the Widget `<script def>`.
For localized metadata, add `app.<BCP-47-locale>.json` at the project root with
`locale` and a `widgets` object keyed by Widget path. Override only
`displayName` and `description`; missing translations fall back to `app.json`.
`onAttach()` and `onDetach()` may run as overlays appear and disappear. Keep
one-time initialization in `onCreate(options)`; do not rely on retaining an instance
after closing the overlay.

Open a declared `overlay` Widget with:

```javascript
window.open('widgets/weather/index?city=hangzhou', '_widget');
```

The path must match the declared Widget. Query parameters reach `onCreate(options)`
as strings, such as `options.city`; no parameters produces `{}`.
This API currently opens Widgets only, not other Pages. Close a completed
overlay with `window.close()`. Do not assume its instance survives closing.

## Permissions

Declare only capabilities the agent uses in `app.json.permissions`:

| Permission | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GEOLOCATION` | Get the current location or watch position changes. |
| `CAMERA` | Access the camera and capture images or video. |
| `RECORD_AUDIO` | Access the microphone and capture audio. |

Names are case-sensitive. Unrecognized names grant no capability, and an API
that requires an undeclared permission fails. A manifest declaration does not
grant operating system permission; handle denial and unavailable hardware at
the API call.

Speech recognition has no separate `SPEECH_RECOGNITION` permission. Microphone
capture still requires `RECORD_AUDIO`; external-audio recognition sessions do
not open the microphone themselves.

## Agent Worker Development

Agent Workers are non-visual scripts for temporary shared state or one-time work. They are not Web Workers.

```json
{
  "agentWorkers": [{
    "name": "sync",
    "script": "workers/sync.js",
    "trigger": { "type": "open" },
    "lifetime": "foreground"
  }]
}
```

| Field | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | Required, non-empty, and unique in the agent. |
| `script` | Required project-relative `.js` or `.ts`; no URL, absolute path, `..`, or backslash. |
| `trigger` | Required; currently only `{ "type": "open" }`. Only one open-trigger Worker is allowed. |
| `lifetime` | Required: `instant` or `foreground`. `background` is reserved and invalid. |

Opening a Page or Widget invokes `onOpen(event)`. Register async work before the callback returns:

```javascript
export default {
  refreshPromise: null,
  onOpen(event) {
    event.waitUntil(this.ensureRefreshed());
  },
  ensureRefreshed() {
    if (!this.refreshPromise) {
      this.refreshPromise = this.refresh().finally(() => {
        this.refreshPromise = null;
      });
    }
    return this.refreshPromise;
  },
  async refresh() {
    this.latestStatus = 'ready';
  },
};
```

Making `onOpen()` async does not automatically extend the event. A running Worker retains values on `this`; values reset when it stops. Persist durable state in storage.

Workers provide timers, Promise, `console`, `performance`, URL/text encoding (including `TextEncoderStream` and `TextDecoderStream`), Web Crypto, WebAssembly, and project ES modules. They do not provide Page/Widget objects, `window`, `document`, `fetch`, routing, rendering, or media capture.

## Modules, Components, and Resources

- Declare custom components in `usingComponents`; use absolute project paths for shared components.
- Reference a concrete component file: `/components/card/index` can resolve `index.ink`, but `/components/card` does not automatically search for that directory entry. Missing entry, script, or configuration files fail loading.
- Component `lifetimes.ready()` runs once after a frame containing that component is presented. Dynamically mounted components wait for their own frame; updates, moves, and redisplay do not repeat ready. A component removed before presentation receives no late ready callback.
- Project-local ES module resolution is consistent across agent, Page, and Agent Worker entry points.
- Keep packaged images, fonts, audio, and data under project paths such as `/assets/icon.png`.
- Prefer source paths and the project packaging workflow; do not assume direct access to arbitrary device files.

## TypeScript and WebAssembly

Use project-supported `.ts` entry points where declared. Keep public values crossing JavaScript boundaries serializable. For WebAssembly, load packaged modules through the supported module/resource workflow rather than browser-only filesystem assumptions.
